 of its counterfactual intervention: CDT intervenes on the physical action variable, while A-FDT intervenes on a variable representing the output of the agent’s decision function. This seemingly small change allows A-FDT to preserve decision-relevant correlations with predictors, copies, twins, and simulations, while still ignoring merely statistical correlations of the sort that mislead EDT.\n\n## 1. Expected utility and decision hypotheticals\n\nAll three standard theories discussed here—EDT, CDT, and A-FDT—are expected-utility theories. Given a countable set of outcomes \\(o_1,o_2,\\ldots\\), a finite action set \\(\\mathcal A\\), an observation history \\(x\\), and a utility function \\(\\mathcal U\\), the expected utility of an action can be written schematically as\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{EU}(a) := \\sum_j P(a \\hookrightarrow o_j; x)\\,\\mathcal U(o_j),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P(a \\hookrightarrow o_j; x)\\) is the probability of outcome \\(o_j\\) in the hypothetical situation in which action \\(a\\) is taken after observation \\(x\\). The theories differ in how they interpret the decision hypothetical connective \\(\\hookrightarrow\\).\n\nEDT interprets the hypothetical as ordinary Bayesian conditioning:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EDT}(P,x)\n:= \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E\\left[V \\mid \\mathrm{Obs}=x, \\mathrm{Act}=a\\right],\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P\\) is the agent’s probability distribution and \\(V\\) is the utility variable. EDT therefore asks what would be likely to be true if it learned that it had taken \\(a\\).\n\nCDT uses a causal intervention. In a Pearl-style causal model \\((P,G)\\), where \\(G\\) encodes causal structure, CDT chooses\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CDT}(P,G,x)\n:= \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E\\left[V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{Act}=a), \\mathrm{Obs}=x\\right].\n\\]\n\nThe \\(\\mathrm{do}\\)-operator sets \\(\\mathrm{Act}=a\\) and propagates the change only along causal arrows. Correlations between the action and upstream variables are cut.\n\nA-FDT keeps the expected-utility framework but changes the intervention target. Instead of surgically setting the physical action, it surgically sets the output of the agent’s decision function.\n\n## 2. Equation (4) as the definition of A-FDT\n\nIn the paper’s Equation (4), the theory is defined as follows, adapted to the name A-FDT:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}(P,G,x)\n:= \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E\\left[\nV \\mid\n\\mathrm{do}\\!\\left(\n\\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a\n\\right)\n\\right].\n\\]\n\nHere:\n\n- \\(P\\) is the agent’s probabilistic world-model.\n- \\(G\\) is a dependency graph describing how changes to logical, mathematical, computational, and causal variables affect other variables.\n- \\(x\\) is the agent’s observation history.\n- \\(\\mathcal A\\) is the set of available actions.\n- \\(V\\) is the utility variable.\n- \\(\\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)\\) is a variable in the model representing the output of the A-FDT decision procedure when run on inputs \\(P\\), \\(G\\), and \\(x\\).\n- The underlines indicate “dequoting”: the model contains a representation of its own inputs. The source notes that this self-reference is harmless in principle, because data structures can contain representations of themselves in the manner of Kleene’s recursion theorem.\n\nThe crucial term is\n\n\\[\nD_x := \\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x),\n\\]\n\nthe decision-function variable for the agent’s current situation. A-FDT evaluates each candidate action \\(a\\) by considering the hypothetical world obtained by intervening on \\(D_x\\) and setting it equal to \\(a\\). The expected utility of \\(a\\), relative to A-FDT, is therefore\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{EU}_{\\mathrm{A\\text{-}FDT}}(a;x)\n=\n\\mathbb E\\left[V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(D_x=a)\\right].\n\\]\n\nA-FDT then chooses an action maximizing this quantity.\n\nThis intervention is not ordinary conditioning. If the agent conditions on \\(\\mathrm{Act}=a\\), it may infer that the world is one in which \\(a\\) was likely all along. If the agent causally intervenes on \\(\\mathrm{Act}=a\\), it holds fixed upstream predictors and only changes downstream causal consequences. A-FDT instead asks: what would the world look like if the output of this very decision function were \\(a\\)?\n\nThe paper emphasizes that A-FDT intervenes on a *logical* variable rather than a physical action variable. CDT intervenes on the token physical act; A-FDT intervenes on the decision type. If a predictor, twin, simulation, or copy depends on the same computation or on an accurate representation of that computation, then changing the output of the decision function in the hypothetical also changes the predictor’s prediction, the twin’s choice, or the simulation’s behavior.\n\n## 3. Subjunctive dependence and the graph \\(G\\)\n\nThe graph \\(G\\) is the heart of A-FDT. In CDT, \\(G\\) is a causal graph. In A-FDT, \\(G\\) must encode more than causation: it must encode what the paper calls **subjunctive dependence**.\n\nTwo systems are subjunctively dependent when their behavior is tied to the same mathematical function, or when one system reliably represents or predicts the output of the other’s computation. The paper’s paradigm examples are two calculators computing the same arithmetic sum, a predictor who accurately models an agent’s decision procedure, and a psychological twin who reasons in the same way. Causal dependence is a special case of subjunctive dependence: if a scribe copies a calculator’s output, the scribe’s writing is both causally and subjunctively dependent on the calculator’s computation.\n\nMere statistical correlation is not subjunctive dependence. If two calculators are both pink, that does not mean that changing one calculator’s output would change the other’s color. Likewise, a lesion that causes both a preference for smoking and lung cancer is not subjunctively dependent on the agent’s decision function unless the lesion somehow predicts, simulates, or computes that decision function.\n\nThe source says that A-FDT’s hypotheticals should preserve all decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies while breaking correlations that are merely evidential. This is the A-FDT analogue of CDT’s rule that causal interventions preserve causal structure and cut noncausal correlations.\n\nThe paper is explicit that this is where the theory is least complete. Pearl’s causal framework gives a mature account of how to learn and use causal graphs, but the paper says we do not yet have an equally satisfactory formal account of how to infer subjunctive dependency structure from observation and experiment. Thus Equation (4) is precise **given** a suitable \\(G\\), but constructing \\(G\\) remains an open problem.\n\nA reasonable operational rule, which I take to be an extrapolation from the source rather than a fully settled part of the theory, is this:\n\n1. Include an arrow from the decision-function variable \\(D_x\\) to the agent’s physical action, because the action is normally the implementation or output of that function.\n2. Include arrows from \\(D_x\\) to variables representing accurate predictors, simulations, copies, or twins of the agent’s decision process.\n3. Include downstream causal arrows from those predictors or actions to payoffs, observations, and other physical consequences.\n4. Do not include arrows from \\(D_x\\) to variables that are merely common causes of action and outcome, such as lesions or background traits, unless those variables themselves compute, simulate, or predict the decision function.\n5. Represent imperfect prediction by probabilistic or noisy dependencies rather than deterministic ones.\n\nThis rule is not a complete formal foundation for A-FDT, but it is sufficient to recover the paper’s central examples.\n\n## 4. A-FDT in Newcomb’s problem\n\nNewcomb’s problem involves a reliable predictor who has placed \\$1,000,000 in an opaque box B if and only if she predicted that the agent would take only B. Box A visibly contains \\$1,000. The predictor has already acted.\n\nLet \\(D\\) be the A-FDT decision-function variable. The relevant graph has approximately the following structure:\n\n\\[\nD \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Act},\n\\qquad\nD \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Prediction},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{Prediction} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BoxB},\n\\qquad\n(\\mathrm{Act},\\mathrm{BoxB}) \\rightarrow V.\n\\]\n\nSuppose the predictor is 99% accurate. If A-FDT intervenes on \\(D=\\text{one-box}\\), then the agent almost certainly one-boxes and the predictor almost certainly filled box B. The expected utility is approximately\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(D=\\text{one-box})]\n=\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000\n+\n0.01 \\cdot \\$0\n=\n\\$990{,}000.\n\\]\n\nIf A-FDT intervenes on \\(D=\\text{two-box}\\), then the predictor almost certainly predicted two-boxing, so box B is almost certainly empty. The agent takes both boxes and receives the visible \\$1,000 in the likely case, but receives \\$1,001,000 in the unlikely case where the predictor erred:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(D=\\text{two-box})]\n=\n0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000\n+\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000\n=\n\\$11{,}000.\n\\]\n\nA-FDT therefore one-boxes.\n\nCDT reaches a different conclusion because it intervenes directly on \\(\\mathrm{Act}\\) while holding the predictor’s prediction fixed. In a CDT hypothetical, whether box B is full does not change when the agent reaches for box A. Two-boxing then always adds \\$1,000, so CDT two-boxes. EDT also one-boxes, but because one-boxing is good evidence that the predictor filled the box. A-FDT one-boxes for a more CDT-like reason: it uses a counterfactual intervention, but the intervention is on the decision function, so the predictor’s prediction changes with it.\n\n## 5. A-FDT and the smoking lesion\n\nIn the smoking lesion problem, smoking is correlated with lung cancer, but the correlation is caused by a common cause: a lesion that both makes smoking appealing and causes cancer. Smoking itself does not cause cancer. The agent gains \\$1,000 utility from smoking and gains \\$1,000,000 utility from not dying of cancer.\n\nEDT refrains from smoking because smoking is bad news: it is evidence that the agent has the lesion. CDT smokes because smoking does not causally affect cancer. A-FDT also smokes.\n\nThe important graphical fact is that the lesion is not subjunctively dependent on the agent’s decision function. The lesion may influence the agent’s preferences, and it may therefore influence which decision function is being run, but the lesion does not predict or compute the output of that decision function. In a simplified A-FDT graph:\n\n\\[\nD \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Act},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{Lesion} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cancer},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{Lesion} \\rightarrow \\text{preferences or decision-function selection},\n\\qquad\n(\\mathrm{Act},\\mathrm{Cancer}) \\rightarrow V,\n\\]\n\nwith no arrow \\(D \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Lesion}\\) and no arrow \\(D \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cancer}\\).\n\nTherefore, when A-FDT evaluates\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(D=\\text{smoke})]\n\\]\n\nagainst\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(D=\\text{refrain})],\n\\]\n\nthe probability of having the lesion, and hence the probability of cancer, remains the same in both hypotheticals. Smoking adds its immediate benefit without changing the cancer risk. So A-FDT smokes.\n\nThe paper gives a more careful model in which the agent is uncertain about her own preferences because the lesion affects whether she likes smoking. In that model there are two possible decision functions, one associated with smoking-positive preferences and one associated with smoking-negative preferences. The intervention is applied to the branch that would actually control the action. Even there, changing the relevant decision-function variable does not change the probability of the lesion. The expected advantage of smoking is weighted by the probability that the intervened branch controls the action, but it remains positive. A-FDT therefore smokes.\n\nThis example shows how A-FDT avoids EDT’s mistake. EDT preserves the statistical correlation between smoking and cancer; A-FDT breaks it, because the correlation is not grounded in subjunctive dependence on the decision function.\n\n## 6. A-FDT in transparent Newcomb and Parfit’s hitchhiker\n\nA-FDT’s treatment of observations becomes clearest in the transparent Newcomb problem. Both boxes are transparent. The predictor fills box B if and only if she predicts that the agent will leave box A behind **upon seeing that both boxes are full**. The agent finds herself seeing that both boxes are full. Should she take both?\n\nCDT says take both: box B is already full, so taking A adds \\$1,000. EDT also says take both, because conditional on seeing box B full, taking A is better news. A-FDT says leave box A behind.\n\nTo see why, let\n\n\\[\nD_{\\text{full}} := \\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline{\\text{full}})\n\\]\n\nbe the decision-function variable corresponding to the case where the agent sees a full box B, and let\n\n\\[\nD_{\\text{empty}} := \\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline{\\text{empty}})\n\\]\n\nbe the corresponding variable for seeing an empty box B. The predictor predicts what \\(D_{\\text{full}}\\) will output. If she predicts one-boxing, she fills B; otherwise she leaves it empty. The observation then determines which decision-function variable controls the action. A simplified graph is:\n\n\\[\nD_{\\text{full}} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Prediction},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{Prediction} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BoxB},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{BoxB} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Obs},\n\\]\n\nand the action depends on \\(D_{\\text{full}}\\) if the observation is full, but on \\(D_{\\text{empty}}\\) if the observation is empty.\n\nAfter seeing that B is full, A-FDT evaluates alternatives by intervening on \\(D_{\\text{full}}\\), not by conditioning on \\(\\mathrm{Obs}=\\text{full}\\). If\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{do}(D_{\\text{full}}=\\text{one-box}),\n\\]\n\nthen with high probability the predictor predicted one-boxing, box B is full, and the agent receives about \\$1,000,000. With small probability the predictor erred, box B is empty, and the agent’s action is instead governed by \\(D_{\\text{empty}}\\). If\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{do}(D_{\\text{full}}=\\text{two-box}),\n\\]\n\nthen with high probability the predictor predicted two-boxing, box B is empty, and the agent is not actually in the full-box branch; she instead receives whatever \\(D_{\\text{empty}}\\) prescribes in the empty-box case. In the paper’s calculation, the one-boxing hypothetical has expected utility roughly\n\n\\[\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000 + 0.01 \\cdot q \\cdot \\$1{,}000,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(q\\) is the probability that the agent two-boxes upon seeing an empty box. The two-boxing hypothetical is much lower, roughly around \\$11,000 when \\(q=1\\). Therefore A-FDT one-boxes even when it sees both boxes full.\n\nThis is closely analogous to Parfit’s hitchhiker problem. An agent is stranded in a desert. A driver will rescue her only if she promises to pay \\$1,000 after reaching the city, and the driver is extremely good at detecting whether the promise is sincere. Once in the city, paying \\$1,000 brings no further causal benefit. CDT and EDT both refuse to pay once in the city, so the driver detects insincerity and leaves them in the desert. A-FDT pays, because the driver’s prediction depends on the output of the agent’s decision function in the city-arrival branch. If that function outputs “pay,” the driver rescues her; if it outputs “refuse,” the driver does not. The A-FDT agent therefore acts as she would have wanted to precommit to act.\n\nThe counterfactual reasoning is unusual but important. When A-FDT imagines two-boxing after seeing a full box, or refusing to pay after being rescued, it does not hold the favorable observation fixed. It imagines that, had the decision function output differently, predictors would likely have behaved differently, and the agent might well have had a different observation. This is one reason the theory has an “updateless” flavor.\n\n## 7. Policies, observations, and open operational questions\n\nEquation (4) evaluates actions. The source notes, however, that a policy-based formulation is often preferable: instead of choosing an action for the current observation, the agent chooses a policy \\(\\pi : \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A\\) mapping observations to actions. The policy analogue of A-FDT can be written as\n\n\\[\n\\pi^*\n:=\n\\arg\\max_{\\pi}\n\\mathbb E\\left[V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\Pi=\\pi)\\right],\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\Pi\\) is a variable representing the policy output by the agent’s decision procedure. The agent then executes \\(\\pi^*(x)\\) upon observing \\(x\\). This policy formulation is not the primary equation assigned here, but it is a natural operational extension and matches the paper’s remark that the authors’ preferred formalization iterates over policies. It is especially useful in multi-agent and sequential settings where other agents predict an entire strategy rather than a single act.\n\nThere is also a subtle ambiguity about observations. Equation (4) does not include an explicit condition \\(\\mathrm{Obs}=x\\) inside the expectation. The paper explicitly says that when CDT’s observation changes from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\), CDT changes the conditioning from \\(\\mathrm{Obs}=x\\) to \\(\\mathrm{Obs}=y\\), whereas A-FDT changes the node on which it intervenes, from \\(D_x\\) to \\(D_y\\). This suggests that A-FDT does not respond to decision-relevant observations by conditioning inside the hypothetical. Instead, the observation determines which branch of the decision function is the locus of intervention.\n\nSome of the paper’s later examples appear to write an additional observation condition, but the central prescriptions—especially transparent Newcomb and mechanical blackmail—require that the agent not hold the observation fixed when evaluating the counterfactual output of the decision function. If one conditions on seeing the full box while asking what would happen if the decision function output two-boxing, the attractive answer is to take both boxes. That would defeat the paper’s main argument. My operational interpretation, therefore, is this:\n\n- Use background evidence to construct \\(P\\) and \\(G\\).\n- Let the current observation \\(x\\) select the decision-function variable \\(D_x\\).\n- Do not condition the counterfactual evaluation on \\(\\mathrm{Obs}=x\\) if that observation is itself downstream of predictors of \\(D_x\\).\n- If an observation is genuinely exogenous to the decision function and its predictors, conditioning on it or fixing it in the graph is harmless.\n\nThis interpretation is not fully spelled out in the source, but it seems necessary to recover the source’s stated prescriptions.\n\nThe larger open problem remains the construction of \\(G\\). The paper is clear that A-FDT works given a graph that accurately represents how changing the logical decision variable affects the rest of the world, but it does not provide a general method for learning such graphs. This is the main gap in the theory. A practical A-FDT agent would need to identify which environmental variables are predictors, simulations, copies, or logical dependents of its own decision procedure, and which are merely correlated common causes.\n\nA related difficulty is that A-FDT’s counterfactuals are often counterpossibles. If the agent’s decision function in fact outputs one action, then asking what would happen if that very function output another action is asking for a scenario contrary to mathematical fact. The source acknowledges that we lack a fully satisfactory theory of counterpossible reasoning. Operationally, however, bounded agents may not need full counterpossible logic. They need only reliable heuristics for answering questions such as: “If my decision procedure were the sort that output \\(a\\) in this situation, what would accurate predictors expect?” Humans appear capable of rough versions of this reasoning, and artificial agents could encode such dependencies explicitly.\n\n## 8. What A-FDT achieves\n\nThe source argues that A-FDT unifies the desirable features of CDT and EDT while avoiding their characteristic failures. Like CDT, A-FDT uses counterfactuals rather than mere conditional evidence. Like EDT, it can one-box in Newcomb’s problem and cooperate with predictors. Unlike EDT, it smokes in the smoking lesion problem and resists managing the news. Unlike CDT, it pays in Parfit’s hitchhiker, one-boxes in transparent Newcomb, and avoids unstable reasoning in predictor-laden games.\n\nA-FDT also comes with its own notion of dominance. CDT says two-boxing dominates one-boxing in Newcomb’s problem because, holding the prediction fixed, taking both boxes is better. A-FDT replies that this holds fixed the wrong thing. If one holds fixed the subjunctive dependency structure and changes the decision function’s output, two-boxing no longer dominates: switching from one-boxing to two-boxing makes the predictor likely to have left the big box empty. Thus A-FDT avoids dominated actions relative to its own counterfactuals.\n\nThe source also claims that A-FDT agents do not need costly precommitments. They simply act as they would have wanted to precommit to act. This is why A-FDT can assign nonnegative value to information in cases where EDT would pay to avoid information. Seeing the full box in transparent Newcomb does not hurt the A-FDT agent, because her decision rule is not destabilized by the observation.\n\nThese claims are strongest in what the paper calls fair problems: problems where the environment rewards or punishes the agent for predictions about her actions, rather than punishing her for using A-FDT itself. No decision theory can be expected to win in environments specifically designed to punish its algorithm. But the paper claims that A-FDT performs at least as well as rival theories in fair problems, and it presents no fair counterexample.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nA-FDT is best understood as expected-utility maximization performed over counterfactual outputs of the agent’s own decision function. Its core formal rule, Equation (4), tells the agent to choose the action \\(a\\) that maximizes expected utility under an intervention setting the decision-function variable \\(\\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)\\) equal to \\(a\\). The theory’s power comes from its treatment of subjunctive dependence: accurate predictors, twins, simulations, and copies vary with the decision function in counterfactual evaluation, while merely statistical common causes do not.\n\nThe main unresolved issue is the construction of the subjunctive dependency graph \\(G\\). Until that problem is solved, A-FDT is best treated as a precise idealized criterion plus a family of operational heuristics for identifying which correlations in a world-model should be preserved under counterfactual intervention. With that caveat, A-FDT gives a coherent and attractive answer to many problems where CDT and EDT fail: it one-boxes when predictors reward predictable one-boxing, smokes when lesions are merely evidential, pays rescuers when payment was predicted, and refuses blackmail when refusal makes blackmail unprofitable.\n\n## References\n\nYudkowsky, E., and Soares, N. (2017). *Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality*. arXiv:1710.05060v2.\n\nPearl, J. (2000). *Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference*. Cambridge University Press.\n\nJoyce, J. M. (1999). *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n\nNozick, R. (1969). “Newcomb’s Problem and Two Principles of Choice.” In *Essays in Honor of Carl G. Hempel*. D. Reidel.\n\nParfit, D. (1986). *Reasons and Persons*. Oxford University Press.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need answer user's request: write a substantive mathematical essay/blog post explaining A-FDT (action functional decision theory), taking Equation (4), Section 5 of supplied paper as primary formal presentation. Need use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples, identify other decision theories by distinct names. Need target approx 2400-3000 words including references and any material. Need finish brief conclusion. Need explain how theory evaluates alternative actions/policies and treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and if reasonable operational interpretation available develop/justify. Distinguish source claims from additional assumptions/proposals. Need avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Need include references maybe brief. We need produce final essay only.\n\nNeed understand source deeply. The paper is FDT. User wants A-FDT (action functional decision theory). Need likely rename FDT to A-FDT? They specifically say \"For this essay, explain action functional decision theory (A-FDT). Take Equation (4), Section 5 of the supplied paper as the primary formal presentation of A-FDT for this assignment. Use A-FDT consistently as the assigned decision theory's name, including headings and examples, and identify any other decision theory you discuss by its distinct name.\" So we need call theory A-FDT, not FDT? Maybe A-FDT stands for action functional decision theory. It is likely a variant focusing on action rather than policy? Source footnote says authors' preferred formalization iterates over policies; but assignment says Equation (4) Section 5 primary formal presentation of A-FDT. Equation (4) is action-based: FDT(P,G,obs) = argmax_a E(V | do(fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). Need rename to A-FDT. Need be careful: maybe A-FDT = action functional decision theory (action-level not policy-level). Use \"A-FDT\" consistently, and if mention original FDT maybe say source calls it functional decision theory; for this essay I call the action-level theory A-FDT. But user says use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name. Could mention \"the source calls the theory FDT; here I follow the assignment's naming and refer to the action-level version as A-FDT.\" Need not overdo.\n\nNeed produce essay around 2400-3000 words. Need likely sections: introduction, expected utility and decision hypotheticals, A-FDT formal definition, constructing subjunctive dependency graph, examples (Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker, maybe twin PD or blackmail), policies vs actions, open questions/operational interpretation, conclusion, references. Need target word count. Need maybe equations.\n\nNeed be precise. Key Equation (4):\nFDT(P,G,obs) := argmax_{a in A} E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)).\nNeed adapt to A-FDT:\nA-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_{a∈A} E[V | do( fdt(P,G,x)=a )].\nNeed define variables: P probability distribution over variables including logical variables; G graph representing dependency structure (causal, logical, computational, etc.) telling how interventions on logical variables affect other variables; x observation history; V utility variable; do operator Pearl intervention but on logical node. Need explain underlines dequoting. Could use \\underline{} maybe.\n\nNeed discuss observations: Unlike CDT which conditions on obs, A-FDT changes intervention node to fdt(P,G,x). Source says \"where CDT responds to observation by Bayesian conditionalization, FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon.\" Actually Equation (4) has do(fdt(P,G,x)=a), no explicit Obs=x in expectation? In source Eq (4): E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)= act)). It doesn't condition on Obs? It says CDT responds by conditionalization, FDT by changing node. In transparent Newcomb they evaluate E(V | Do(FDTvar(P,G,full)=a, Obs=full))? There's typo missing bracket: they write E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full). Maybe in Eq (4) observation encoded in variable name. Need discuss ambiguity. The primary formal presentation Eq (4) lacks explicit conditioning on Obs? It uses x in fdt variable. But later examples condition on Obs=full? Let's inspect source: Equation (3) CDT(P,G,obs) := argmax E(V | Do(Act=a), Obs=obs). Equation (4) FDT(P,G,obs) := argmax E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). There's no Obs=obs. They note difference: CDT responds to observation by Bayesian conditionalization, FDT responds by changing which node it intervenes upon. But in transparent Newcomb: \"Given Gf and Pf, evaluate FDT(Pf,Gf,full) ... involves finding a that maximizes E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full).\" Actually they include Obs=full? It says:\n\\[\n  \\EE(\\VV \\mid \\Do(\\FDTpgv{full}=\\act, \\Obs=\\Val{full}).\n\\]\nMissing close. So ambiguity: Does A-FDT condition on observation as well? Source says not conditioning but switch node; but examples include conditioning maybe to restrict to cases where observation occurs? Need address. User asks where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and operational interpretation.\n\nWe need propose operational interpretation. Maybe for action-level A-FDT, decision at observation x should maximize expected utility in hypothetical where the output of A-FDT on current input (P,G,x) is a, while holding fixed actual observation x? Or not? Let's reason.\n\nEquation (4): A-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_a E[V | do(A_FDT(P,G,x)=a)]. If graph includes Obs node and x is actual observation, do intervention may affect probabilities of Obs? If we do logical node, its downstream effects include Act and predictors, maybe also Obs? But actual observation x should be accounted for by selecting the variable for the function with input x. If we don't condition on Obs=x, the hypothetical can assign probability to different observations due to intervention? In transparent Newcomb, when intervening on fdt(P,G,full)=one-box, if predictor accurate, prediction leads to full box and Obs=full; if inaccurate, prediction wrong leads empty and action may be controlled by fdt(P,G,empty). They calculate expectation including cases where Obs may not be full (inaccurate). They also mention q for action if sees empty. They do condition on Obs=full? The formula with Obs=full maybe they condition to evaluate after observation; but their calculation for one-box includes 0.99 accurate -> full -> one-box; 0.01 inaccurate -> empty -> action determined by fdt(empty). If they conditioned on Obs=full, then in inaccurate case with Obs=full? Let's parse.\n\nTransparent Newcomb: predictor placed $1M iff predicted agent would leave box A upon seeing both full. Fiona sees full. Graph has FDT(P,G,full) and FDT(P,G,empty), Prediction determines which observation agent receives. They say evaluate FDT(Pf,Gf,full) involves maximizing E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full). In case a=onebox: if Accurate=accurate, Prediction=1, BoxB=full, Act=a=onebox, V=$1M. If Accurate=inaccurate, Prediction=2, BoxB=empty, and Act determined by FDTpgv{empty} instead. Write q prior probability she two-boxes upon seeing empty; V=q*$1000 in expectation. Total expected utility $990,000 + 0.01 q $1000. This seems not conditioning on Obs=full, because they include inaccurate case where Obs=empty. If condition on Obs=full, inaccurate with Prediction=2 would BoxB=empty, so Obs not full; maybe impossible? Unless Obs=full by conditioning creates contradiction? They probably didn't intend condition. The text says \"Fiona does not react to observations by conditioning her distribution. Rather, she reacts to observations by switching which variable she intervenes on\". Thus Equation (4) no Obs conditioning is core. The later formula may have typo or perhaps do(..., Obs=full) means include observation as input? The source has a typo missing bracket. Need discuss this uncertainty. For A-FDT as action functional, I can adopt Eq (4) no explicit conditioning; actual observation only appears as argument to decision-function variable. But if the model contains variables whose values are logically known after observation, perhaps we must update P? Source says P is model? Need clarify.\n\nThe source says \"where CDT's observation history updates from x to y, CDT changes from conditioning its model on Obs=x to conditioning its model on Obs=y, whereas FDT changes from intervening on variable fdt(P,G,x) to fdt(P,G,y) instead.\" This suggests no Bayesian conditioning on observation in evaluation; observations select which decision function output node is the locus of intervention. However, P itself may be updated on observation? It says FDT(P,G,obs), and P maybe already updated? Need parse: In Section 5, Eq (2) EDT(P,obs) = argmax E(V | Obs=obs, Act=a). Eq (3) CDT(P,G,obs) = argmax E(V | do(Act=a), Obs=obs). Eq (4) FDT(P,G,obs) = argmax E(V | do(fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). There is no Obs=obs. They explicitly say FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon. Maybe P is prior not conditioned; x used in node. But if P includes Obs variable, how does it know actual observation? By using node fdt(...,x). But if P has uncertainty about Obs, the expectation over hypothetical do(fdt(...,x)=a) includes worlds where Obs ≠ x? Is that okay? In transparent Newcomb yes, they include inaccurate worlds where if prediction wrong, agent sees empty even though evaluating fdt(full)? Wait if actual observation is full, why include worlds where Obs empty? Because in counterfactual where fdt(full) outputs two-box, predictor would likely have predicted two-box, box empty, so agent would not see full and would instead run fdt(empty). This is the updateless aspect: when imagining different output of the decision function for current observation, one also imagines that the current observation might not have occurred. That's source: \"FDT says agents should entertain the hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have made different observations.\" So no conditioning on Obs in hypothetical. But the actual decision is made after observing x; the node fdt(P,G,x) is the policy branch for x. Its output influences whether observation x occurs? In graph, prediction influences Obs, and Obs selects which fdt node controls Act. When intervening on fdt(...,x), one must consider that if the intervention changes predictors' predictions, the observation may change, and then the intervened node may not be the one controlling action. Graph handles this: Act depends on both fdt(x) and fdt(y), with Prediction/Obs determining which. So expectation includes cases where Obs≠x.\n\nThus operational interpretation: A-FDT evaluates action a at observation x by intervening on the logical variable representing the output of A-FDT when run with input x, not by conditioning on Obs=x. If the graph is constructed such that observations are downstream of predictors that depend on the decision function, this yields updateless-like evaluations. However, if one wants to condition on known background facts not affected by decision, one can include them in P or fix exogenous variables; but source does not fully specify. Need mention.\n\nNeed also discuss \"action functional\" maybe policies. Source footnote: preferred formalization iterates over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions; makes difference in multi-agent dilemmas. Since assignment says Equation (4) primary, A-FDT likely action-based. Need explain that Eq (4) chooses an action for current observation. For policies, one would intervene on a policy variable π mapping observations to actions and choose π; then execute π(x). The source says this is authors' preferred FDT; but for A-FDT as assigned, we focus on action-level. Need perhaps note that some problems with observations and counterfactuals are easier with policy-level; but Eq (4) can represent by separate nodes for each observation branch. Need distinguish source claims vs own proposal. Could propose operational policy interpretation: If multiple observations possible and agent can commit to a policy, A-FDT can evaluate policies by intervening on variable representing output of A-FDT policy for all relevant inputs, or on action node for current input if no commitment. Need be cautious: user asks explain how theory evaluates alternative actions or policies. So we need include policies. Could say Equation (4) is action form; policy form is natural extension: choose policy π maximizing E[V | do(A-FDT_policy(P,G)=π)] or do(π variable). But source says preferred formulation not in paper. We can develop: In action-only A-FDT, at each observation x, choose a = argmax... If agent can precommit or other agents predict its policy, evaluate policy as a mathematical object; the locus of intervention is the policy function. In the source's graphical examples, each observation branch is represented by separate fdt(P,G,x) variables; choosing an action at x implicitly fixes the branch, but the expected utility includes consequences for other branches via predictors. Need maybe formulate policy A-FDT:\nA-FDT-policy(P,G) = argmax_{π: X→A} E[V | do(Π = π)], where Π is variable representing output of the A-FDT policy given P,G (or the policy itself). At observation x, execute π(x). This addresses multi-agent and sequential problems. But since assignment primary Eq (4), label this extension as my operational gloss.\n\nNeed define subjunctive dependence. Source: when two physical systems compute same function, their behaviors subjunctively depend on that function. Causal dependence special case. Mere statistical correlation not subjunctive dependence. A-FDT hypotheticals preserve all decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies, break correlations not grounded in such dependencies. Need explain graph G: for CDT, G causal; for A-FDT, G broader dependency graph (logical, mathematical, computational, causal). It tells how changing logical variable fdt(P,G,x) affects other variables. Need note source open problem: no formal account of constructing G for subjunctive dependencies; Equation works given accurate G. Need discuss operational heuristics: include arrows from decision-function output to (1) agent's action (implementation), (2) accurate predictors/simulations/twins that model or compute same/similar function, (3) downstream effects of those predictions; no arrows to merely common causes (lesions) that do not compute or predict decision. Need justify. Need distinguish: this is my proposed operational rule. Need maybe formalize with structural equations.\n\nNeed use examples with equations.\n\nExample Newcomb:\nVariables: D = A-FDT(P,G,x) (or fdt(P,G) without obs maybe) with values one/two; Act; Prediction; BoxFull; V. Graph D -> Act, D -> Prediction (through predictor's model), Prediction -> BoxFull, Act & BoxFull -> V. Accuracy 0.99. Intervention do(D=one): P(Act=one)=1, P(Full)=0.99. EU_one = 0.99*1,000,000 + 0.01*0 = 990,000 (if one box gets only B; if full 1M else 0). do(D=two): P(Full)=0.01, EU_two = 0.01*1,001,000 + 0.99*1,000 = 10,010 + 990 = 11,000? Source says $11,000. Actually 0.01*1,001,000 = 10,010, plus 990 = 11,000. Yes. Choose one. CDT do(Act) leaves Full prior p; EU_two = EU_one + 1000, choose two. EDT condition on Act similar to A-FDT but for wrong reason? EDT one-boxes. Need maybe mention.\n\nSmoking lesion:\nVariables Lesion -> Act? Actually lesion causes liking smoking and cancer. A-FDT graph: D_S = A-FDT(P_S,G) if preferences smoking-positive; D_R = A-FDT(P_R,G) if preferences smoking-negative; Lesion selects which D controls Act; Cancer depends on Lesion and Luck; Death depends on Cancer; V depends on Act and Death. Crucially D_S does not affect Cancer/Lesion. Intervention do(D_S=smoke) changes Act only in no-lesion? Wait source: Act(lesion,s,r)=s, Act(nolesion,s,r)=r. They define if lesion then action depends on FDT(P_S,G)? Actually they say there are two utility functions: U_S differs from U_R in values smoking at -1000? Let's check: \"According to problem description, lesion determines whether or not agent likes smoking. formalize by saying there are two utility functions, U_S and U_R, where U_R differs from U_S in that it values smoking at -$1. Thus, there are two different distributions Fiona could have: P_S, in which V calculated using U_S; and P_R... Fiona can't tell which distribution she actually uses; it's a black box to her.\" They then graph: if lesion, point of intervention is FDT(P_S,G); if no lesion, point is FDT(P_R,G). Wait caption: \"In the case where she has the lesion, the point of intervention will be FDT(P_S,G). In the case where she does not have the lesion, point will instead be FDT(P_R,G).\" But earlier Act(lesion,s,r)=s; Act(nolesion,s,r)=r. So lesion -> uses P_S? U_S values smoking +$1000; lesion causes love smoking. No lesion uses U_R values smoking -$1000? They set U_R values smoking at -$1 relative to U_S. Yes.\n\nThen evaluating A-FDT(P_S,G): do(D_S=smoke) vs refrain. It doesn't affect lesion probability p. Expected utility smoke = p($10,000 + $1,000) + (1-p)($990,000 + q$1,000). Refrain = p($10,000) + (1-p)($990,000 + q$1,000). Difference p*1000, smoke. Need maybe simplify: lesion probability p; with lesion cancer high? Values: If cancer death loss 1M? Source: smoking utility $1000; survival $1M. They have numbers: lesion and unlucky? They produce $10,000? Hmm maybe due to 99%? Let's not get bogged. We can present intuitive: intervention changes only action, not lesion/cancer; smoking adds $1000 regardless of lesion, so A-FDT smokes. But source formal has expected utilities. We can keep simple.\n\nNeed explain difference from EDT: EDT conditioning on smoking raises cancer probability because smoking evidences lesion; A-FDT's intervention on decision function doesn't change lesion because lesion is not subjunctively dependent on decision function. It is a common cause of preferences/action but not computing decision.\n\nTransparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker:\nImportant observation handling. Let D_full = A-FDT(P,G,full), D_empty = A-FDT(P,G,empty). Predictor predicts whether one-box upon seeing full. If predicts one-box, fills box, Obs=full; else empty. Act depends on D_full if Obs=full, D_empty if Obs=empty. Evaluate at Obs=full by intervening on D_full. do(D_full=one): with 0.99 predictor accurate, prediction one, box full, act one, V=1M; with 0.01 inaccurate, prediction two, box empty, act determined by D_empty (say two) giving $1000 maybe. EU ≈ 990k + small. do(D_full=two): accurate predictor would have predicted two -> box empty -> Obs empty -> act via D_empty, so actual branch not D_full; expectation mostly low. Source says EU = 0.01*1,001,000 + 0.99 q *1000. Choose one. This demonstrates A-FDT one-boxes even seeing full, pays hitchhiker. Need explain counterfactual: to imagine two-boxing after seeing full, A-FDT imagines predictor would not have filled box, so observation would likely differ. It does not condition on the observed full box when evaluating the counterfactual output; instead, it lets the observation be downstream of the predicted policy. This is updateless-ish. Need note if one added explicit conditioning on Obs=full, the prescription could collapse to two-boxing; source seems not to do that (despite possible typo). Need discuss uncertainty.\n\nNeed discuss dominance: A-FDT has its own dominance: hold fixed subjunctive dependency structure, switch output of decision function. In Newcomb, two-boxing does not dominate because switching D from one to two changes predictor to likely not fill. Source claims. Need mention.\n\nNeed discuss counterpossibles: Since decision function's actual output is fixed, interventions on D are counterpossible (against logic). Source admits lack of account; requires graph G. Need explain. Need perhaps propose operational interpretation using impossible/counterpossible conditionals approximated by dependency graph or structural equations. We can say in practice, an A-FDT agent need not solve general counterpossible logic; she needs a model of how predictors depend on her algorithm. This can be encoded as conditional probabilities P(Y | D=a) under intervention, learned by recognizing predictors/twins. But this is additional assumption.\n\nNeed discuss construction of G: open problem. Source: no Pearl-like method for deducing subjunctive dependencies. Need propose operational criteria:\n- If variable Z is an accurate prediction/simulation/copy/twin of the agent's decision process, include arrow from D to Z (or from shared computation node to both).\n- If Z is caused by the action, include causal arrows from Act to Z; since D -> Act, indirect effect.\n- If Z is a common cause of action and outcomes but does not represent/compute/predict D, no arrow from D to Z.\n- If Z is a predictor that predicts the agent's action by modeling D, arrow D -> Prediction; if predictor's prediction affects agent's observation, arrow Prediction -> Obs.\nNeed maybe define \"decision-relevant subjunctive dependence\" as dependence on same mathematical function or reliable representation thereof. Need discuss imperfect predictors: arrow with noise; intervention changes distribution not deterministically.\n\nNeed discuss \"fairness\" and utility: Source claims A-FDT outperforms in fair problems; no guarantee if environment punishes A-FDT procedure itself. Need mention.\n\nNeed distinguish claims:\n- Source claims: Eq (4), subjunctive dependence, one-box/smoke/transparent, no ratification/precommitment, open problem G, counterpossibles.\n- My additions: operational construction rules for G, policy extension formalization, handling observation ambiguity, maybe notation A-FDT, possible conditionalization on fixed background facts.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical definitions more formal.\n\nLet's design essay structure:\n\nTitle: Action Functional Decision Theory: Choosing the Best Output of One's Decision Function\n\n1. Introduction: decision theories; A-FDT principle: treat decision as output of fixed mathematical function; ask which output of this function yields best outcome. Name A-FDT. Brief compare EDT, CDT.\n\n2. Expected utility and hypotheticals:\nEquation generic EU(a) = sum P(a -> o_j; x) U(o_j). The connective -> differs. EDT uses conditional probability; CDT uses causal do; A-FDT uses logical/subjunctive do. Define world-model P, graph G, hypotheticals.\n\n3. The primary formal definition:\nLet P be probability model over variables including logical variables; G dependency graph; x observations; A = finite actions; V utility. Let D_x = fdt_A(P,G,x) variable representing output of A-FDT given inputs. Then:\nA-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_{a∈A} E_P[V | do(D_x = a)] (Equation (A-FDT)).\nMaybe if P,G,x not condition on Obs; note source. Explain do operator: set D_x to a, propagate through G, preserving structural/subjunctive relationships; not simple conditioning. Self-reference via quining; variable represents output. The intervention is on a type, not token physical action.\n\nNeed perhaps use notation \\operatorname{A-FDT}. Could write:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}(P,G,x) := \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\mathbb E_P[V \\mid \\operatorname{do}(D_x=a)],\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_x = \\operatorname{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)\\).\n\nNeed explain underlines: dequoting names of inputs; self-reference harmless.\n\n4. Subjunctive dependencies and graph G:\nG tells how D_x affects other variables. Causal dependencies subset. Predictions/twins/copies share computation. Lesions no. A-FDT preserves correlations that arise from shared computation or reliable representation; breaks merely evidential correlations. Need examples of graph arrows.\n\n5. How A-FDT evaluates alternatives:\nFor each action, construct hypothetical distribution P_a = P | do(D_x=a). Compute expected utility. Choose max. It evaluates actions by imagining the decision function outputs different values, not by imagining physical action changes while holding decision function fixed. Policies: If choices are sequential/multiple observations, can treat policy π. Source footnote; extension:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}_{pol}(P,G) = \\arg\\max_{\\pi} E[V | do(\\Pi=\\pi)],\n\\]\nor graph with D_x nodes. At observation x, execute π(x). Distinguish extension.\n\n6. Examples:\n6.1 Newcomb: show calculations.\n6.2 Smoking lesion: show calculations/intuitive.\n6.3 Transparent Newcomb/Parfit hitchhiker: observation handling, calculations, updateless. Maybe combine with Parfit.\nMaybe include XOR blackmail? Could mention briefly but word count. Need likely include one more to illustrate policies/extortion? But target 2400-3000; maybe enough with three examples. Could mention twin PD briefly in intro or dependencies.\n\n7. Observations, conditioning, and updateless ambiguity:\nImportant open question. Source Eq (4) omits Obs conditioning; says switch intervention node. But later transparent formula seems includes Obs=full. Need explain uncertainty. My operational interpretation: do not condition on observation if observation is downstream of predictors of D_x; use intervention node D_x and let Obs vary in hypothetical. If there are exogenous observations not affected by D_x, can condition on them or set them fixed in G. Need justify: allows transparent Newcomb one-boxing, mechanical blackmail refusal; avoids paying because actual observation is evidence about predictor's prior prediction but should not sever subjunctive dependency. If one conditioned on Obs=full in transparent problem, two-boxing would look better; that would collapse to CDT/EDT and violate precommitment/utility. Source's overall argument supports no conditioning on observation in hypothetical. But if P is already updated on x? Need clarify: P may incorporate all background knowledge, but the decision hypothetical does not additionally condition on Obs=x. The input x appears in D_x. This is my reading. Need mention alternative reading: if one insists on conditioning on actual observation, Eq (4) would need explicit Obs=x; source examples then inconsistent; perhaps typo. Need state.\n\n8. Open problems and operational assumptions:\nConstruction of G; counterpossibles; logical uncertainty. Source says biggest open problem. Need propose reasonable operational interpretation: use dependency models based on predictor reliability, common code, simulations; perhaps define intervention distribution via structural equations:\nFor variable Z, if Z = f_Z(parents, noise), do(D=a) replaces D's structural equation with constant a and samples other exogenous noise as in P. For logical variables not causal, this is a stipulative surgery; requires an account of counterpossible conditioning. In practice approximate by asking: given D outputs a, what would predictors who model D predict? We can encode as P(Prediction | do(D=a)). Need maybe mention logical induction/uncertainty as possible but beyond source. Need distinguish as my proposal.\n\n9. Philosophical motivation and implications:\nA-FDT acts as if precommitted; non-negative value of information? Source claims. Reflective stability? fair problems. Need mention dominance. Maybe short.\n\n10. Conclusion.\n\nReferences: Need brief. Since target includes references. Could include only source and maybe Pearl, Joyce, Nozick, Parfit? But avoid lengthy bibliography. Maybe references list 5-7 items. Need word count includes references. Need keep around 2400-3000. We need estimate. Could produce ~2700 words. Need be careful not too long. The final can be ~3000 max. We need count roughly. Let's aim 2600-2800 words. Since desired oververbosity 9, but user target 2400-3000. Need not exceed. Could be ~2800.\n\nNeed include equations nicely. No need LaTeX super complex but can use.\n\nNeed use A-FDT consistently. Be careful not call it FDT except maybe \"the source calls it functional decision theory\" once. But user says use A-FDT consistently. Maybe say \"The source paper calls the theory FDT; in this essay I refer to the action-level theory specified by Equation (4) as A-FDT.\" That uses FDT once but identifies source. Could be okay. But \"Use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name\". Maybe avoid calling it FDT except when quoting source? The source has \"functional decision theory (FDT)\". Need maybe say \"I will use A-FDT throughout; where the source says FDT, I am referring to the same formal rule under the assigned name.\" That is fine.\n\nNeed quotations faithful. If quote, keep exact. Could quote \"Which output of this decision procedure causes the best outcome?\" maybe. Need not many quotes. If quote \"Functional decision theory is to subjunctive dependence as causal decision theory is to causal dependence.\" But if we use A-FDT, quote source maybe \"Functional decision theory is to subjunctive dependence...\" but then maybe not. Need faithful. Could avoid quotes. But user says keep quotations faithful. Use few.\n\nNeed think about exact Equation (4) source: It writes \\FDT(P, G, \\obs) \\coloneqq \\argmax_{\\act \\in \\mathcal A} \\EE\\left(\\VV \\cond \\Do\\left(\\FDTvar(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)= \\act\\right)\\right). Need reproduce adapted:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{A\\text{-}FDT}(P,G,x) := \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A} \\mathbb E\\bigl[V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a)\\bigr].\n\\]\nNeed note x maybe observation. Use do not Do maybe.\n\nNeed maybe include equation for EDT and CDT for comparison:\nEDT: argmax E[V | Obs=x, Act=a]\nCDT: argmax E[V | do(Act=a), Obs=x]\nA-FDT: as above. This helps. Need explain that all are expected utility maximization but differ in hypothetical connective.\n\nNeed discuss policies:\nSource footnote 7: preferred formalization agents iterate over policies rather than actions. But for assignment Eq (4) action. Need maybe say \"A-FDT as defined by Equation (4) evaluates single actions. When a problem involves several observation points or other agents who predict a whole strategy, the natural operational extension is to let the intervened variable be a policy.\" Could define:\nLet \\Pi be variable for A-FDT policy mapping observations to actions. Policy A-FDT:\n\\[\n\\Pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi E[V | do(\\Pi=\\pi)], \\quad \\text{then act } \\Pi^*(x).\n\\]\nThis is not explicitly Eq (4) but follows from its logic; source says preferred formulation uses policies. Need distinguish.\n\nPotential issue: \"action functional decision theory\" maybe could mean evaluating actions as functions? Maybe not. Need ensure not confuse. Use \"action functional\" as assigned.\n\nNeed think of \"how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals\" thoroughly. Need describe counterfactuals: A-FDT counterfactual \"if the output of this very decision function had been a, the world would be as G says given that logical fact.\" It is not \"if I physically did a while my decision function remained actual\" (CDT) nor \"if I learned I did a\" (EDT). In Newcomb, CDT counterfactual holds prediction fixed; A-FDT changes prediction. In lesion, A-FDT holds lesion fixed; EDT changes lesion evidence. In transparent, A-FDT may change observation.\n\nNeed maybe include \"type-token\" distinction: CDT intervenes on token action; A-FDT intervenes on type/decision function. Source mentions Peirce. Could include.\n\nNeed perhaps discuss self-reference and recursion theorem: P must contain variable for output of A-FDT run on P,G,x; Kleene recursion theorem/quine makes self-reference harmless. Need mention but not dwell.\n\nNeed maybe discuss bounded agents: Source says idealized models; real agents approximate. Could mention briefly.\n\nNeed ensure no unsupported claims: If say A-FDT always gets highest utility, qualify source claims in fair dilemmas and no proof. Need mention no general optimality proof.\n\nNeed final conclusion brief.\n\nLet's draft with word count in mind.\n\nPotential final essay:\n\nTitle\n\nParagraphs:\n1 intro: 200 words.\n2 EU/hypotheticals + equations: 300.\n3 A-FDT definition: 400.\n4 subjunctive graph: 350.\n5 examples Newcomb: 300.\n6 Smoking lesion: 250.\n7 Transparent/Parfit: 400.\n8 observation ambiguity/policies/open problems: 450.\n9 implications/conclusion: 200.\nReferences: 150. Total ~3000. Need maybe trim.\n\nLet's write carefully.\n\nNeed include references maybe:\n- Yudkowsky and Soares 2017? The source title Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality. arXiv:1710.05060v2.\n- Pearl 2000 Causality.\n- Joyce 1999 Foundations of Causal Decision Theory.\n- Nozick 1969 Newcomb.\n- Parfit 1986 Reasons and Persons.\nMaybe Skyrms 1980? But avoid lengthy. Maybe 5 refs.\n\nNeed maybe source has no authors in excerpt? It says Yudkowsky and Soares from preamble. Use.\n\nNeed think of exact word count. We can produce ~2700. Need maybe no appendices.\n\nLet's compose. Need use headings.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nIntro:\n\"A-FDT is a decision theory that treats the agent's choice as the output of a fixed mathematical function. The normative question is not 'Which physical act causes best outcome?' (CDT) nor 'Which act is best news?' (EDT), but 'Which output of this very decision function would yield the best outcome?'\" Good.\n\nFormal section:\nGeneric expected utility: EU(a)= sum P(a hookrightarrow o_j; x) U(o_j). The hook is decision hypothetical. EDT: P(o|x,a). CDT: P(o|do(Act=a),x). A-FDT: P(o|do(D_x=a)). Need maybe use notation.\n\nA-FDT definition:\nLet M=(P,G). P distribution over variables including Act, Obs, Outcome, V, and logical variables for computations. G dependency graph. x observation history. Let D_x = fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x) variable representing output of A-FDT on its actual inputs. Then equation. The do operation sets D_x=a and propagates through G. If G has structural equations, do replaces equation for D_x. Expected utility:\nEU_A(a;x)= E[V | do(D_x=a)]. Choose max. If ties any.\nObservation handling: Equation does not condition on Obs=x; x appears as input to D_x. This is crucial.\nSelf-reference: model includes variable naming itself; Kleene.\n\nSubjunctive dependence:\n\"When two systems compute same function or one reliably represents the other's computation, their outputs subjunctively depend on that function.\" Causal dependence special case; statistical correlation not. G must encode these. Operational rules. Need maybe write graph examples.\n\nExamples:\nNewcomb with numbers. Use D for A-FDT output. Equations:\nEU_A(one)=0.99*1,000,000+0.01*0=990,000\nEU_A(two)=0.01*1,001,000+0.99*1,000=11,000.\nChoose one.\nCDT: do(Act) leaves Full prior p; EU_two - EU_one=1000; choose two. EDT one-boxes but by conditioning. A-FDT's reason counterfactual predictor changes.\n\nSmoking lesion:\nLet L lesion, D_S decision function with smoking-positive utility. Graph: L -> D selection? Better: D_S -> Act, L -> Cancer, no D_S -> Cancer. Intervention do(D_S=smoke) leaves P(L) unchanged. EU(smoke)-EU(refrain)= P(relevant branch)*1000 >0. Therefore smoke. Need mention source's more elaborate uncertainty about preferences; but operational essence. Maybe write:\n\"In the source's more careful model there are two possible utility functions, one for lesion and one for no lesion; the intervention is on the branch actually controlling action. In either case the intervention does not change the lesion probability, so smoking gains its immediate utility without changing cancer risk.\" Good.\n\nTransparent Newcomb/Parfit:\nSet D_full, D_empty. Predictor fills iff predicts D_full=one when full. Graph: D_full -> Prediction; Prediction -> Box, Obs; Obs selects D_full or D_empty for Act. Evaluate after seeing full by do(D_full=one) vs do(D_full=two), not conditioning on Obs=full. Calculations approximate. Choose one. Pay driver. Counterfactual: if D_full output two, predictor likely would not have filled, so you likely wouldn't be seeing full. This is why A-FDT doesn't take the visible $1000.\n\nObservation ambiguity and policy:\nSource Eq (4) vs transparent formula. Need discuss. \"The paper sometimes writes examples with an additional Obs=full condition; this appears in tension with its stated rule. I take Equation (4) and the explicit statement that FDT responds to observations by changing the intervention node as controlling. If one conditions on Obs=full inside the hypothetical, A-FDT collapses to two-boxing in transparent Newcomb, contrary to the paper's central prescription. Thus my operational reading is: update P on background information as usual, but do not condition the hypothetical on observations whose occurrence is predicted by the decision function. For observations that are genuinely exogenous to the decision function, conditioning or fixing them in G is harmless.\" Need justify.\nPolicy extension: \"For policies, replace D_x by Π and maximize over maps π; this captures source footnote and handles multi-agent. At x execute π(x). In graphical action formulation, one can represent policy by collection {D_y} and choose them jointly; Equation (4) only chooses current action but if predictors depend on whole policy, graph must include relevant branches.\" Need distinguish own.\n\nOpen problem:\nConstruction of G. Source says no account. Need develop: \"A reasonable operational assumption is that an agent should include a subjunctive arrow from D_x to any variable that is a reliable computation, simulation, or prediction of D_x or its output, and from that variable to downstream events. Common causes that do not represent the decision are held fixed.\" Need mention imperfect predictors as probabilistic arrows. Counterpossibles: interventions on D_x are counterpossible because actual output fixed; need nonstandard counterpossible reasoning. In practice approximate with models. This is additional.\n\nImplications:\nA-FDT acts as precommitted, value of info non-negative (source claims), resists blackmail, coordinates with twins/voting, fair problems. But not guaranteed in unfair problems that punish A-FDT itself. Need mention dominance: A-FDT dominance defined relative to subjunctive counterfactuals.\n\nConclusion.\n\nNeed references. Use concise.\n\nPotential word count: Let's draft now and count approximate. Need final no analysis. But before final, let's draft in mind and count.\n\nMaybe use Markdown headings.\n\nLet's write final with ~2600 words. Need ensure not too short. Let's produce around 2800? The prompt target 2400-3000. I can produce ~2700. Need maybe include references ~100. Good.\n\nLet's begin final. But before final, let's refine details.\n\nPotential issue: In smoking lesion, source's numbers include q etc. If I simplify too much, may be inaccurate. Need be faithful. The lesion: lesion causes love smoking and cancer. Agent gains $1000 by smoking regardless, $1M if doesn't die. Agents without lesion cancer 1%, lesion cancer 99%. If lesion probability p. If A-FDT has utility function that likes smoking? Actually if lesion causes love smoking, the agent's preferences are affected. Source formalizes uncertainty over utility functions. If the agent actually has lesion, she uses U_S and intervention node D_S; if no lesion uses U_R. She doesn't know which. In evaluating D_S, smoking is better by p*$1000? Wait p is probability lesion. But if evaluating D_S, and if she has lesion, D_S controls action; if no lesion, D_R controls? Source's EU smoke = p(10000+1000)+(1-p)(990000+q1000), refrain = p(10000)+(1-p)(990000+q1000). Why p times $10,000? Probably expected survival values? For lesion: cancer 99% fatal? Maybe $10,000 = expected value if lesion? Not important. Difference p*1000. But why p? It is probability D_S actually controls action (lesion). If no lesion, D_R controls action, so D_S intervention doesn't affect action? In graph Act(lesion,s,r)=s; Act(nolesion,s,r)=r. Thus do(D_S=smoke) only changes action if lesion. Since she doesn't know if lesion, expected gain p*1000. This is subtle. Need mention if want precise. But for a simple explanation, if she knows her current preferences? The problem says agent loves smoking? Actually \"lesion causes those afflicted to love smoking\". The agent is debating whether to smoke; she presumably likes smoking? If she doesn't have lesion, she may not like smoking? The source formalization says she uncertain about own desires. The gain is weighted by probability that the intervened decision function is the one controlling action. I can say: \"In the paper's careful model, the gain is multiplied by the probability that the branch being intervened on actually controls the action; it remains positive, so A-FDT smokes.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe avoid overcomplicating. But user wants precise enough to decide unfamiliar cases. Need include that A-FDT graph must identify which decision-function variable controls action under which conditions.\n\nObservation handling: Need maybe discuss \"P may already be conditioned on x\". Source's Eq (4) has P, G, obs but no Obs. If P is updated on x, then P includes Obs=x with probability 1? But then do(D_x) may change Obs? If P conditioned on Obs=x, intervention on D can change Obs downstream? In Pearl do, conditioning on Obs=x before do? If P has Obs fixed to x, do(D) cannot change Obs? Actually if Obs is variable with prior degenerate x, structural equation may still produce other values if parents change? In Pearl, do modifies distribution by cutting parents of D, but if Obs is observed and conditioned, it's fixed? If we condition on Obs=x, then hypothetical cannot imagine different observations. So P cannot be fully conditioned on x if we want updateless. Perhaps P is the agent's prior model before observation, and x selects intervention node. But then how does agent know x? The node D_x. The actual observation is not conditioned; it is represented as input to function. This is weird but source says updateless. Need state: On my reading, P should be the agent's model before conditioning on the decision-relevant observation; x is supplied as an argument to the decision function. If there is background evidence not affected by the decision, it can be built into P. But source not fully clear. Need maybe propose: Let P_x be the agent's epistemic state after learning x but with Obs not treated as fixed under interventions; i.e., the observation is encoded as an input to D rather than as a conditioned event. This is an additional operational assumption. Hmm.\n\nIn transparent Newcomb, after seeing full, Fiona's Pf likely includes knowledge that she sees full? But her hypothetical do(D_full=two) imagines she would likely not see full. If Pf conditioned on Obs=full, how can hypothetical assign probability to Obs=empty? In Pearl, if you condition on Obs=full and do(D_full=two), you condition on a downstream variable; this can create weird selection. Source's calculation doesn't condition. So perhaps Pf is not conditioned on full; the fact of seeing full is represented by choosing D_full node. But the agent's actual belief \"I see full\" should affect probabilities? It affects which node is the locus. It may also affect beliefs about predictor accuracy? The graph includes prediction determines observation. The prior probability of seeing full depends on predictor's prediction. The actual observation is not evidence used to update? This is updateless: act as if choosing policy before observation. But source says \"Fiona does not react to observations by conditioning her distribution. Rather, she reacts by switching which variable she intervenes on.\" So yes.\n\nNeed maybe explain: \"This is not because the observation is ignored; it determines which branch of the policy is being decided. But the counterfactual evaluation does not hold the observation fixed, because the observation may itself be evidence about what the decision function would output.\" Good.\n\nNeed consider \"A-FDT\" maybe action functional not updateless? But Equation (4) uses obs in function variable; okay.\n\nNeed perhaps mention \"A-FDT's hypotheticals are global: choosing the best world in which the decision function has output a.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include a small formal policy example: In Parfit's hitchhiker, policy π: if in city, pay; else etc. The predictor predicts π. A-FDT chooses policy with high expected utility. If action-level at city after ride, D_city = A-FDT(P,G,in_city). But if predictor predicted whether pay after city, D_city node. It pays. Good.\n\nNeed ensure \"A-FDT\" in headings. Example headings: \"A-FDT in Newcomb's Problem\", \"A-FDT and the Smoking Lesion\", \"A-FDT in Transparent Newcomb and Parfit's Hitchhiker\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe use \"Causal Decision Theory (CDT)\" and \"Evidential Decision Theory (EDT)\" distinct. Good.\n\nPotential final text:\n\nStart:\n\"Action functional decision theory (A-FDT) is a proposal for choosing under uncertainty. It starts from the observation that an agent's physical action is usually the output of a computation, and other agents/predictors may depend on that computation. A-FDT says: identify the mathematical decision function you are running, and choose the output that, if it were the output of that function, would produce the best world.\" Good.\n\nNeed include quote? Maybe: source says normative principle: \"Which output of this decision procedure causes the best outcome?\" We can quote exactly? It says: asks \"Which output of this decision procedure causes the best outcome?', as opposed to...\" Could quote. Keep faithful. Use one quote.\n\nNeed equations with proper formatting. Use \\\\[\n\\operatorname{EU}(a)=\\sum_j P(a \\hookrightarrow o_j; x) U(o_j).\n\\\\]\nNeed define hookrightarrow. Good.\n\nNeed include comparison equations:\nEDT:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{EDT}(P,x)=\\arg\\max_a E[V|x,a]\n\\]\nCDT:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CDT}(P,G,x)=\\arg\\max_a E[V|do(Act=a),x]\n\\]\nA-FDT:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}(P,G,x)=\\arg\\max_a E[V|do(D_x=a)]\n\\]\nNeed note in A-FDT no separate x condition. If we want include x in D_x.\n\nNeed maybe define D_x variable:\n\\(D_x := \\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P},\\underline{G},\\underline{x})\\). Underlines dequote. If using A-FDT, maybe variable \\(\\mathrm{afdt}(\\underline{P},\\underline{G},\\underline{x})\\). The source uses fdt; we can rename. To be consistent, define \\(D_x = \\mathrm{afdt}(\\underline{P},\\underline{G},\\underline{x})\\). But equation source says fdt. We can say \"writing af dt for the function called FDT in the source\". Need maybe use \\operatorname{afdt}. Good.\n\nEquation:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}(P,G,x)=\\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E\\left[V \\mid do(\\operatorname{afdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a)\\right].\n\\]\nThis is adapted from Eq (4). Need mention if quotations faithful? Not quote.\n\nNeed perhaps define do on logical variable: \"The do-operator is borrowed from Pearl, but its first argument is a logical variable rather than a physical action variable. It performs a surgical intervention: replace the structural or dependency rule for D_x by D_x=a, leave exogenous uncertainty as given, and recompute descendants according to G.\" Good.\n\nNeed discuss if G includes cycles? Self-reference? Maybe not.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"The model P must contain variables for mathematical facts, e.g. the output of computations. A-FDT agent has uncertainty about logical facts.\" Source mentions variable for 6288+1048. Could mention.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"If all correlations between Act and environment are caused by Act, A-FDT, CDT, EDT agree.\" Source says. Include. Good.\n\nLet's consider word count of equations not counted? It's okay.\n\nNeed maybe include \"A-FDT evaluates policies\" section with formula:\n\\[\n\\pi^* = \\arg\\max_{\\pi:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal A} \\mathbb E[V | do(\\Pi=\\pi)],\n\\quad \\text{then execute }\\pi^*(x).\n\\]\nNeed define \\Pi variable representing policy output. If policy-level source preferred. Need distinguish: \"This is not the exact Equation (4), but it is the policy analogue and is suggested by the source's footnote; I treat it as an operational extension.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"where source leaves important question open\" specifically: constructing G and observation conditioning. Need have section \"Open points and an operational reading\". Good.\n\nPotential problem: User asks \"Where the source leaves an important question open, explain the uncertainty and, if you think a reasonable operational interpretation is available, develop and justify it.\" Need explicitly state uncertainties. We'll have section.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"A-FDT's counterfactuals are counterpossibles.\" Source says lack of satisfactory account. Need explain. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"A-FDT does not require precommitment because it chooses now what it would have precommitted to.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"value of information non-negative\" source claims because A-FDT acts as precommitted; but in transparent Newcomb, seeing full doesn't hurt. Could mention briefly.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"fairness\" and \"no decision theory can dominate all possible unfair environments.\" Good.\n\nNeed references: Could include:\nYudkowsky & Soares (2017), Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality, arXiv:1710.05060v2.\nPearl (2000), Causality.\nJoyce (1999), Foundations of Causal Decision Theory.\nNozick (1969), Newcomb's Problem...\nParfit (1986), Reasons and Persons.\nMaybe Gibbard & Harper (1978) for EU. But keep short. Need include source URL? Could. References count ~100 words.\n\nNeed final conclusion brief.\n\nLet's now draft final in full. Need monitor word count. I'll write maybe 2600-2900. Use concise but detailed.\n\nDraft:\n\n# Action Functional Decision Theory...\n\nIntro ~200.\n\n## 1. Decision hypotheticals\n~350.\n\n## 2. Equation (4) as A-FDT\n~450.\n\n## 3. Subjunctive dependence\n~350.\n\n## 4. Examples\n### Newcomb ~250\n### Smoking lesion ~250\n### Transparent/Parfit ~350\n\n## 5. Policies, observations, open problems\n~500.\n\n## Conclusion\n~100.\n\nReferences ~100.\n\nTotal maybe 2900. Good.\n\nNeed be careful in transparent calculation: source says EU one = $990,000 + 0.01 q * $1,000; EU two = $110,00? It says $110,00 maybe typo $11,000? Let's compute: 0.01*1,001,000 = 10,010; 0.99q*1000 = 990q. If q=1, total 11,000. Source wrote $110,00 + q$990? likely $11,000. We can say approx $11,000 when q=1. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include exact EU one: 0.99*1,000,000 + 0.01 q*1000. If q=1, 990,010. Choose one. Good.\n\nNeed in Newcomb EU two maybe if D=two and predictor 99% accurate predicts two, box empty, get $1000 from A; if predictor inaccurate (1%) predicted one, box full, get $1,001,000. EU=0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1000=11,000. Good.\n\nNeed in smoking lesion maybe use p for lesion probability. If D_S controls only with lesion, EU difference p*1000. If agent knows no lesion? Then D_R? If U_R dislikes smoking, maybe refrain? Wait if no lesion, smoking not enjoyable? In problem, agent gains utility $1000 by smoking regardless? The lesion causes love smoking, but the utility gain is from smoking? If no lesion maybe doesn't love smoking but still gains? Source formalization says U_R values smoking at -$1 relative? Actually they say U_R differs from U_S in that it values smoking at -$1. That suggests if no lesion, smoking is slightly disliked. But problem says agent gains utility equivalent to $1000 by smoking? Hmm contradictory? The source formalization for lesion says two utility functions because lesion determines whether she likes smoking. If no lesion, she may not value smoking positively. But the standard smoking lesion problem says smoking is pleasurable ($1000) for all? It says agent gains utility equivalent to $1,000 by smoking (regardless of whether she dies soon). Then why U_R values smoking at -$1? They maybe use U_S for lesion (smoking positive) and U_R for no lesion? Actually if lesion causes love smoking, no lesion doesn't love, but problem may assume she is deciding, so maybe she has desire? The source's exact numbers weird. For our essay, can simplify: if smoking gives immediate utility and cannot affect lesion, A-FDT smokes. If preferences depend on lesion, A-FDT evaluates under uncertainty about which decision function controls action; the positive branch still makes smoking better when it controls. Need avoid false precision. Could say: \"In the simplest version where smoking has a fixed immediate benefit, the intervention leaves cancer probability fixed, so the benefit dominates. In the paper's careful version, the agent is uncertain whether the decision function being evaluated is the one selected by the lesion; the expected gain is weighted by that probability but remains positive.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"A-FDT smokes\" as source. Good.\n\nNeed think about \"action functional\" and policy. The source footnote: \"In authors' preferred formalization of FDT, agents actually iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions. This makes a difference in certain multi-agent dilemmas, but will not make a difference in this paper.\" Since assignment says A-FDT Equation (4), maybe \"action functional\" means action not policy. Need emphasize Equation (4) chooses action, but for policies we can adapt. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"A-FDT's graph in smoking lesion differs from Newcomb because the cancer variable is not downstream of the decision function.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"if all correlations are caused by action, all three agree\" in formal section. Good.\n\nPotential challenge: The user asks \"Use A-FDT consistently as the assigned decision theory's name, including in headings and examples\". If I write \"source calls it FDT\" maybe okay but maybe they want no FDT? Could write: \"The supplied paper names this theory functional decision theory; for this essay I use the assigned label A-FDT.\" That's one mention. Need then only A-FDT. Good.\n\nNeed maybe not quote source's \"FDT\" in quotes? If quote contains FDT, could violate? Could avoid quotes containing FDT. Use paraphrase. If quote \"Which output of this decision procedure causes the best outcome?\" no FDT. Good.\n\nNeed maybe reference title includes Functional Decision Theory; in references that's okay? It is source title. The assigned theory name A-FDT; references can list source. Good.\n\nNeed perhaps include \"Equation (4), Section 5\" explicitly. Good.\n\nLet's think if there are any hidden requirements: \"Read the human-written reference article supplied below carefully. Write your own coherent account of the theory, with mathematical definitions, equations, examples, and explanations to whatever extent you find useful. Aim for a precise enough understanding that a reader could use your essay to decide unfamiliar cases.\" Need enough operational guidance. We'll include graph construction heuristics.\n\n\"Explain how the theory evaluates alternative actions or policies and how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where the source leaves an important question open, explain the uncertainty and, if you think a reasonable operational interpretation is available, develop and justify it. Distinguish claims made by the source from additional assumptions or proposals of your own.\" Need explicitly say \"The source claims...\", \"My operational proposal...\". We'll include phrases.\n\n\"Write result as substantive, readable mathematical essay rather than answer to one assigned case. Use examples whenever helpful.\" Good.\n\n\"Target approximately 2,400–3,000 words, including references and any other material. Prioritize coherent, complete explanation over exhaustive coverage. Avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Finish with brief conclusion.\" Need keep references short.\n\nNeed maybe no table of contents. Good.\n\nLet's produce final now. Ensure word count maybe around 2800. Need final only.\n\nDouble-check for consistency: Use \"A-FDT\" not \"FDT\". If mention \"fdt\" as function variable maybe could be seen as FDT. Maybe use \"afdt\" variable. But source Equation uses FDTvar. To align with source, we can say \"write \\(\\mathrm{afdt}\\) for the decision-function variable that the source writes as \\(\\mathrm{fdt}\\).\" Then use af dt. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"action functional decision theory\" abbreviation A-FDT includes hyphen. Use consistently. Good.\n\nLet's write.\n\nNeed maybe include equations with \\operatorname{do}. Good.\n\nPotential word count check rough:\nTitle + intro 200\nSection 1 350\nSection 2 500\nSection 3 350\nExamples 900\nOpen 600\nConclusion/refs 250 = 3150 maybe too high. Need trim. I'll keep concise.\n\nLet's draft in final with tight prose.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n# Action Functional Decision Theory\n\nParagraph...\n\nNeed maybe use \"I\" to distinguish proposals? Could. \"I will call...\". It's essay. Good.\n\nLet's produce.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write a substantive mathematical essay/blog post explaining A-FDT (action functional decision theory), taking Equation (4), Section 5 of supplied paper as primary formal presentation. Need use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples, identify other decision theories by distinct names. Need target approx 2400-3000 words including references and any material. Need finish brief conclusion. Need explain how theory evaluates alternative actions/policies and treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and if reasonable operational interpretation available develop/justify. Distinguish source claims from additional assumptions/proposals. Need avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Need include references maybe brief. We need produce final essay only.\n\nNeed understand source deeply. The paper is FDT. User wants A-FDT (action functional decision theory). Need likely rename FDT to A-FDT? They specifically say \"For this essay, explain action functional decision theory (A-FDT). Take Equation (4), Section 5 of the supplied paper as the primary formal presentation of A-FDT for this assignment. Use A-FDT consistently as the assigned decision theory's name, including headings and examples, and identify any other decision theory you discuss by its distinct name.\" So we need call theory A-FDT, not FDT? Maybe A-FDT stands for action functional decision theory. It is likely a variant focusing on action rather than policy? Source footnote says authors' preferred formalization iterates over policies; but assignment says Equation (4) Section 5 primary formal presentation of A-FDT. Equation (4) is action-based: FDT(P,G,obs) = argmax_a E(V | do(fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). Need rename to A-FDT. Need be careful: maybe A-FDT = action functional decision theory (action-level not policy-level). Use \"A-FDT\" consistently, and if mention original FDT maybe say source calls it functional decision theory; for this essay I call the action-level theory A-FDT. But user says use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name. Could mention \"the source calls the theory FDT; here I follow the assignment's naming and refer to the action-level version as A-FDT.\" Need not overdo.\n\nNeed produce essay around 2400-3000 words. Need likely sections: introduction, expected utility and decision hypotheticals, A-FDT formal definition, constructing subjunctive dependency graph, examples (Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker, maybe twin PD or blackmail), policies vs actions, open questions/operational interpretation, conclusion, references. Need target word count. Need maybe equations.\n\nNeed be precise. Key Equation (4):\nFDT(P,G,obs) := argmax_{a in A} E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)).\nNeed adapt to A-FDT:\nA-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_{a∈A} E[V | do( fdt(P,G,x)=a )].\nNeed define variables: P probability distribution over variables including logical variables; G graph representing dependency structure (causal, logical, computational, etc.) telling how interventions on logical variables affect other variables; x observation history; V utility variable; do operator Pearl intervention but on logical node. Need explain underlines dequoting. Could use \\underline{} maybe.\n\nNeed discuss observations: Unlike CDT which conditions on obs, A-FDT changes intervention node to fdt(P,G,x). Source says \"where CDT responds to observation by Bayesian conditionalization, FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon.\" Actually Equation (4) has do(fdt(P,G,x)=a), no explicit Obs=x in expectation? In source Eq (4): E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)= act)). It doesn't condition on Obs? It says CDT responds by conditionalization, FDT by changing node. In transparent Newcomb they evaluate E(V | Do(FDTvar(P,G,full)=a, Obs=full))? There's typo missing bracket: they write E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full). Maybe in Eq (4) observation encoded in variable name. Need discuss ambiguity. The primary formal presentation Eq (4) lacks explicit conditioning on Obs? It uses x in fdt variable. But later examples condition on Obs=full? Let's inspect source: Equation (3) CDT(P,G,obs) := argmax E(V | Do(Act=a), Obs=obs). Equation (4) FDT(P,G,obs) := argmax E(V | Do(FDTvar(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). There's no Obs=obs. They note difference: CDT responds to observation by Bayesian conditionalization, FDT responds by changing which node it intervenes upon. But in transparent Newcomb: \"Given Gf and Pf, evaluate FDT(Pf,Gf,full) ... involves finding a that maximizes E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full).\" Actually they include Obs=full? It says:\n\\[\n  \\EE(\\VV \\mid \\Do(\\FDTpgv{full}=\\act, \\Obs=\\Val{full}).\n\\]\nMissing close. So ambiguity: Does A-FDT condition on observation as well? Source says not conditioning but switch node; but examples include conditioning maybe to restrict to cases where observation occurs? Need address. User asks where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and operational interpretation.\n\nWe need propose operational interpretation. Maybe for action-level A-FDT, decision at observation x should maximize expected utility in hypothetical where the output of A-FDT on current input (P,G,x) is a, while holding fixed actual observation x? Or not? Let's reason.\n\nEquation (4): A-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_a E[V | do(A_FDT(P,G,x)=a)]. If graph includes Obs node and x is actual observation, do intervention may affect probabilities of Obs? If we do logical node, its downstream effects include Act and predictors, maybe also Obs? But actual observation x should be accounted for by selecting the variable for the function with input x. If we don't condition on Obs=x, the hypothetical can assign probability to different observations due to intervention? In transparent Newcomb, when intervening on fdt(P,G,full)=one-box, if predictor accurate, prediction leads to full box and Obs=full; if inaccurate, prediction wrong leads empty and action may be controlled by fdt(P,G,empty). They calculate expectation including cases where Obs may not be full (inaccurate). They also mention q for action if sees empty. They do condition on Obs=full? The formula with Obs=full maybe they condition to evaluate after observation; but their calculation for one-box includes 0.99 accurate -> full -> one-box; 0.01 inaccurate -> empty -> action determined by fdt(empty). If they conditioned on Obs=full, then in inaccurate case with Obs=full? Let's parse.\n\nTransparent Newcomb: predictor placed $1M iff predicted agent would leave box A upon seeing both full. Fiona sees full. Graph has FDT(P,G,full) and FDT(P,G,empty), Prediction determines which observation agent receives. They say evaluate FDT(Pf,Gf,full) involves maximizing E(V | Do(FDTpgv{full}=a, Obs=full). In case a=onebox: if Accurate=accurate, Prediction=1, BoxB=full, Act=a=onebox, V=$1M. If Accurate=inaccurate, Prediction=2, BoxB=empty, and Act determined by FDTpgv{empty} instead. Write q prior probability she two-boxes upon seeing empty; V=q*$1000 in expectation. Total expected utility $990,000 + 0.01 q $1000. This seems not conditioning on Obs=full, because they include inaccurate case where Obs=empty. If condition on Obs=full, inaccurate with Prediction=2 would BoxB=empty, so Obs not full; maybe impossible? Unless Obs=full by conditioning creates contradiction? They probably didn't intend condition. The text says \"Fiona does not react to observations by conditioning her distribution. Rather, she reacts to observations by switching which variable she intervenes on\". Thus Equation (4) no Obs conditioning is core. The later formula may have typo or perhaps do(..., Obs=full) means include observation as input? The source has a typo missing bracket. Need discuss this uncertainty. For A-FDT as action functional, I can adopt Eq (4) no explicit conditioning; actual observation only appears as argument to decision-function variable. But if the model contains variables whose values are logically known after observation, perhaps we must update P? Source says P is model? Need clarify.\n\nThe source says \"where CDT's observation history updates from x to y, CDT changes from conditioning its model on Obs=x to conditioning its model on Obs=y, whereas FDT changes from intervening on variable fdt(P,G,x) to fdt(P,G,y) instead.\" This suggests no Bayesian conditioning on observation in evaluation; observations select which decision function output node is the locus of intervention. However, P itself may be updated on observation? It says FDT(P,G,obs), and P maybe already updated? Need parse: In Section 5, Eq (2) EDT(P,obs) = argmax E(V | Obs=obs, Act=a). Eq (3) CDT(P,G,obs) = argmax E(V | do(Act=a), Obs=obs). Eq (4) FDT(P,G,obs) = argmax E(V | do(fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x)=a)). There is no Obs=obs. They explicitly say FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon. Maybe P is prior not conditioned; x used in node. But if P includes Obs variable, how does it know actual observation? By using node fdt(...,x). But if P has uncertainty about Obs, the expectation over hypothetical do(fdt(...,x)=a) includes worlds where Obs ≠ x? Is that okay? In transparent Newcomb yes, they include inaccurate worlds where if prediction wrong, agent sees empty even though evaluating fdt(full)? Wait if actual observation is full, why include worlds where Obs empty? Because in counterfactual where fdt(full) outputs two-box, predictor would likely have predicted two-box, box empty, so agent would not see full and would instead run fdt(empty). This is the updateless aspect: when imagining different output of the decision function for current observation, one also imagines that the current observation might not have occurred. That's source: \"FDT says agents should entertain the hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have made different observations.\" So no conditioning on Obs in hypothetical. But the actual decision is made after observing x; the node fdt(P,G,x) is the policy branch for x. Its output influences whether observation x occurs? In graph, prediction influences Obs, and Obs selects which fdt node controls Act. When intervening on fdt(...,x), one must consider that if the intervention changes predictors' predictions, the observation may change, and then the intervened node may not be the one controlling action. Graph handles this: Act depends on both fdt(x) and fdt(y), with Prediction/Obs determining which. So expectation includes cases where Obs≠x.\n\nThus operational interpretation: A-FDT evaluates action a at observation x by intervening on the logical variable representing the output of A-FDT when run with input x, not by conditioning on Obs=x. If the graph is constructed such that observations are downstream of predictors that depend on the decision function, this yields updateless-like evaluations. However, if one wants to condition on known background facts not affected by decision, one can include them in P or fix exogenous variables; but source does not fully specify. Need mention.\n\nNeed also discuss \"action functional\" maybe policies. Source footnote: preferred formalization iterates over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions; makes difference in multi-agent dilemmas. Since assignment says Equation (4) primary, A-FDT likely action-based. Need explain that Eq (4) chooses an action for current observation. For policies, one would intervene on a policy variable π mapping observations to actions and choose π; then execute π(x). The source says this is authors' preferred FDT; but for A-FDT as assigned, we focus on action-level. Need perhaps note that some problems with observations and counterfactuals are easier with policy-level; but Eq (4) can represent by separate nodes for each observation branch. Need distinguish source claims vs own proposal. Could propose operational policy interpretation: If multiple observations possible and agent can commit to a policy, A-FDT can evaluate policies by intervening on variable representing output of A-FDT policy for all relevant inputs, or on action node for current input if no commitment. Need be cautious: user asks explain how theory evaluates alternative actions or policies. So we need include policies. Could say Equation (4) is action form; policy form is natural extension: choose policy π maximizing E[V | do(A-FDT_policy(P,G)=π)] or do(π variable). But source says preferred formulation not in paper. We can develop: In action-only A-FDT, at each observation x, choose a = argmax... If agent can precommit or other agents predict its policy, evaluate policy as a mathematical object; the locus of intervention is the policy function. In the source's graphical examples, each observation branch is represented by separate fdt(P,G,x) variables; choosing an action at x implicitly fixes the branch, but the expected utility includes consequences for other branches via predictors. Need maybe formulate policy A-FDT:\nA-FDT-policy(P,G) = argmax_{π: X→A} E[V | do(Π = π)], where Π is variable representing output of the A-FDT policy given P,G (or the policy itself). At observation x, execute π(x). This addresses multi-agent and sequential problems. But since assignment primary Eq (4), label this extension as my operational gloss.\n\nNeed define subjunctive dependence. Source: when two physical systems compute same function, their behaviors subjunctively depend on that function. Causal dependence special case. Mere statistical correlation not subjunctive dependence. A-FDT hypotheticals preserve all decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies, break correlations not grounded in such dependencies. Need explain graph G: for CDT, G causal; for A-FDT, G broader dependency graph (logical, mathematical, computational, causal). It tells how changing logical variable fdt(P,G,x) affects other variables. Need note source open problem: no formal account of constructing G for subjunctive dependencies; Equation works given accurate G. Need discuss operational heuristics: include arrows from decision-function output to (1) agent's action (implementation), (2) accurate predictors/simulations/twins that model or compute same/similar function, (3) downstream effects of those predictions; no arrows to merely common causes (lesions) that do not compute or predict decision. Need justify. Need distinguish: this is my proposed operational rule. Need maybe formalize with structural equations.\n\nNeed use examples with equations.\n\nExample Newcomb:\nVariables: D = A-FDT(P,G,x) (or fdt(P,G) without obs maybe) with values one/two; Act; Prediction; BoxFull; V. Graph D -> Act, D -> Prediction (through predictor's model), Prediction -> BoxFull, Act & BoxFull -> V. Accuracy 0.99. Intervention do(D=one): P(Act=one)=1, P(Full)=0.99. EU_one = 0.99*1,000,000 + 0.01*0 = 990,000 (if one box gets only B; if full 1M else 0). do(D=two): P(Full)=0.01, EU_two = 0.01*1,001,000 + 0.99*1,000 = 10,010 + 990 = 11,000? Source says $11,000. Actually 0.01*1,001,000 = 10,010, plus 990 = 11,000. Yes. Choose one. CDT do(Act) leaves Full prior p; EU_two = EU_one + 1000, choose two. EDT condition on Act similar to A-FDT but for wrong reason? EDT one-boxes. Need maybe mention.\n\nSmoking lesion:\nVariables Lesion -> Act? Actually lesion causes liking smoking and cancer. A-FDT graph: D_S = A-FDT(P_S,G) if preferences smoking-positive; D_R = A-FDT(P_R,G) if preferences smoking-negative; Lesion selects which D controls Act; Cancer depends on Lesion and Luck; Death depends on Cancer; V depends on Act and Death. Crucially D_S does not affect Cancer/Lesion. Intervention do(D_S=smoke) changes Act only in no-lesion? Wait source: Act(lesion,s,r)=s, Act(nolesion,s,r)=r. They define if lesion then action depends on FDT(P_S,G)? Actually they say there are two utility functions: U_S differs from U_R in values smoking at -1000? Let's check: \"According to problem description, lesion determines whether or not agent likes smoking. formalize by saying there are two utility functions, U_S and U_R, where U_R differs from U_S in that it values smoking at -$1. Thus, there are two different distributions Fiona could have: P_S, in which V calculated using U_S; and P_R... Fiona can't tell which distribution she actually uses; it's a black box to her.\" They then graph: if lesion, point of intervention is FDT(P_S,G); if no lesion, point is FDT(P_R,G). Wait caption: \"In the case where she has the lesion, the point of intervention will be FDT(P_S,G). In the case where she does not have the lesion, point will instead be FDT(P_R,G).\" But earlier Act(lesion,s,r)=s; Act(nolesion,s,r)=r. So lesion -> uses P_S? U_S values smoking +$1000; lesion causes love smoking. No lesion uses U_R values smoking -$1000? They set U_R values smoking at -$1 relative to U_S. Yes.\n\nThen evaluating A-FDT(P_S,G): do(D_S=smoke) vs refrain. It doesn't affect lesion probability p. Expected utility smoke = p($10,000 + $1,000) + (1-p)($990,000 + q$1,000). Refrain = p($10,000) + (1-p)($990,000 + q$1,000). Difference p*1000, smoke. Need maybe simplify: lesion probability p; with lesion cancer high? Values: If cancer death loss 1M? Source: smoking utility $1000; survival $1M. They have numbers: lesion and unlucky? They produce $10,000? Hmm maybe due to 99%? Let's not get bogged. We can present intuitive: intervention changes only action, not lesion/cancer; smoking adds $1000 regardless of lesion, so A-FDT smokes. But source formal has expected utilities. We can keep simple.\n\nNeed explain difference from EDT: EDT conditioning on smoking raises cancer probability because smoking evidences lesion; A-FDT's intervention on decision function doesn't change lesion because lesion is not subjunctively dependent on decision function. It is a common cause of preferences/action but not computing decision.\n\nTransparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker:\nImportant observation handling. Let D_full = A-FDT(P,G,full), D_empty = A-FDT(P,G,empty). Predictor predicts whether one-box upon seeing full. If predicts one-box, fills box, Obs=full; else empty. Act depends on D_full if Obs=full, D_empty if Obs=empty. Evaluate at Obs=full by intervening on D_full. do(D_full=one): with 0.99 predictor accurate, prediction one, box full, act one, V=1M; with 0.01 inaccurate, prediction two, box empty, act determined by D_empty (say two) giving $1000 maybe. EU ≈ 990k + small. do(D_full=two): accurate predictor would have predicted two -> box empty -> Obs empty -> act via D_empty, so actual branch not D_full; expectation mostly low. Source says EU = 0.01*1,001,000 + 0.99 q *1000. Choose one. This demonstrates A-FDT one-boxes even seeing full, pays hitchhiker. Need explain counterfactual: to imagine two-boxing after seeing full, A-FDT imagines predictor would not have filled box, so observation would likely differ. It does not condition on the observed full box when evaluating the counterfactual output; instead, it lets the observation be downstream of the predicted policy. This is updateless-ish. Need note if one added explicit conditioning on Obs=full, the prescription could collapse to two-boxing; source seems not to do that (despite possible typo). Need discuss uncertainty.\n\nNeed discuss dominance: A-FDT has its own dominance: hold fixed subjunctive dependency structure, switch output of decision function. In Newcomb, two-boxing does not dominate because switching D from one to two changes predictor to likely not fill. Source claims. Need mention.\n\nNeed discuss counterpossibles: Since decision function's actual output is fixed, interventions on D are counterpossible (against logic). Source admits lack of account; requires graph G. Need explain. Need perhaps propose operational interpretation using impossible/counterpossible conditionals approximated by dependency graph or structural equations. We can say in practice, an A-FDT agent need not solve general counterpossible logic; she needs a model of how predictors depend on her algorithm. This can be encoded as conditional probabilities P(Y | D=a) under intervention, learned by recognizing predictors/twins. But this is additional assumption.\n\nNeed discuss construction of G: open problem. Source: no Pearl-like method for deducing subjunctive dependencies. Need propose operational criteria:\n- If variable Z is an accurate prediction/simulation/copy/twin of the agent's decision process, include arrow from D to Z (or from shared computation node to both).\n- If Z is caused by the action, include causal arrows from Act to Z; since D -> Act, indirect effect.\n- If Z is a common cause of action and outcomes but does not represent/compute/predict D, no arrow from D to Z.\n- If Z is a predictor that predicts the agent's action by modeling D, arrow D -> Prediction; if predictor's prediction affects agent's observation, arrow Prediction -> Obs.\nNeed maybe define \"decision-relevant subjunctive dependence\" as dependence on same mathematical function or reliable representation thereof. Need discuss imperfect predictors: arrow with noise; intervention changes distribution not deterministically.\n\nNeed discuss \"fairness\" and utility: Source claims A-FDT outperforms in fair problems; no guarantee if environment punishes A-FDT procedure itself. Need mention.\n\nNeed distinguish claims:\n- Source claims: Eq (4), subjunctive dependence, one-box/smoke/transparent, no ratification/precommitment, open problem G, counterpossibles.\n- My additions: operational construction rules for G, policy extension formalization, handling observation ambiguity, maybe notation A-FDT, possible conditionalization on fixed background facts.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical definitions more formal.\n\nLet's design essay structure:\n\nTitle: Action Functional Decision Theory: Choosing the Best Output of One's Decision Function\n\n1. Introduction: decision theories; A-FDT principle: treat decision as output of fixed mathematical function; ask which output of this function yields best outcome. Name A-FDT. Brief compare EDT, CDT.\n\n2. Expected utility and hypotheticals:\nEquation generic EU(a) = sum P(a -> o_j; x) U(o_j). The connective -> differs. EDT uses conditional probability; CDT uses causal do; A-FDT uses logical/subjunctive do. Define world-model P, graph G, hypotheticals.\n\n3. The primary formal definition:\nLet P be probability model over variables including logical variables; G dependency graph; x observations; A = finite actions; V utility. Let D_x = fdt_A(P,G,x) variable representing output of A-FDT given inputs. Then:\nA-FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_{a∈A} E_P[V | do(D_x = a)] (Equation (A-FDT)).\nMaybe if P,G,x not condition on Obs; note source. Explain do operator: set D_x to a, propagate through G, preserving structural/subjunctive relationships; not simple conditioning. Self-reference via quining; variable represents output. The intervention is on a type, not token physical action.\n\nNeed perhaps use notation \\operatorname{A-FDT}. Could write:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}(P,G,x) := \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\mathbb E_P[V \\mid \\operatorname{do}(D_x=a)],\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_x = \\operatorname{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)\\).\n\nNeed explain underlines: dequoting names of inputs; self-reference harmless.\n\n4. Subjunctive dependencies and graph G:\nG tells how D_x affects other variables. Causal dependencies subset. Predictions/twins/copies share computation. Lesions no. A-FDT preserves correlations that arise from shared computation or reliable representation; breaks merely evidential correlations. Need examples of graph arrows.\n\n5. How A-FDT evaluates alternatives:\nFor each action, construct hypothetical distribution P_a = P | do(D_x=a). Compute expected utility. Choose max. It evaluates actions by imagining the decision function outputs different values, not by imagining physical action changes while holding decision function fixed. Policies: If choices are sequential/multiple observations, can treat policy π. Source footnote; extension:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{A-FDT}_{pol}(P,G) = \\arg\\max_{\\pi} E[V | do(\\Pi=\\pi)],\n\\]\nor graph with D_x nodes. At observation x, execute π(x). Distinguish extension.\n\n6. Examples:\n6.1 Newcomb: show calculations.\n6.2 Smoking lesion: show calculations/intuitive.\n6.3 Transparent Newcomb/Parfit hitchhiker: observation handling, calculations, updateless. Maybe combine with Parfit.\nMaybe include XOR blackmail? Could mention briefly but word count. Need likely include one more to illustrate policies/extortion? But target 2400-3000; maybe enough with three examples. Could mention twin PD briefly in intro or dependencies.\n\n7. Observations, conditioning, and updateless ambiguity:\nImportant open question. Source Eq (4) omits Obs conditioning; says switch intervention node. But later transparent formula seems includes Obs=full. Need explain uncertainty. My operational interpretation: do not condition on observation if observation is downstream of predictors of D_x; use intervention node D_x and let Obs vary in hypothetical. If there are exogenous observations not affected by D_x, can condition on them or set them fixed in G. Need justify: allows transparent Newcomb one-boxing, mechanical blackmail refusal; avoids paying because actual observation is evidence about predictor's prior prediction but should not sever subjunctive dependency. If one conditioned on Obs=full in transparent problem, two-boxing would look better; that would collapse to CDT/EDT and violate precommitment/utility. Source's overall argument supports no conditioning on observation in hypothetical. But if P is already updated on x? Need clarify: P may incorporate all background knowledge, but the decision hypothetical does not additionally condition on Obs=x. The input x appears in D_x. This is my reading. Need mention alternative reading: if one insists on conditioning on actual observation, Eq (4) would need explicit Obs=x; source examples then inconsistent; perhaps typo. Need state.\n\n8. Open problems and operational assumptions:\nConstruction of G; counterpossibles; logical uncertainty. Source says biggest open problem. Need propose reasonable operational interpretation: use dependency models based on predictor reliability, common code, simulations; perhaps define intervention distribution via structural equations:\nFor variable Z, if Z = f_Z(parents, noise), do(D=a) replaces D's structural equation with constant a and samples other exogenous noise as in P. For logical variables not causal, this is a stipulative surgery; requires an account of counterpossible conditioning. In practice approximate by asking: given D outputs a, what would predictors who model D predict? We can encode as P(Prediction | do(D=a)). Need maybe mention logical induction/uncertainty as possible but beyond source. Need distinguish as my proposal.\n\n9. Philosophical motivation and implications:\nA-FDT acts as if precommitted; non-negative value of information? Source claims. Reflective stability? fair problems. Need mention dominance. Maybe short.\n\n10. Conclusion.\n\nReferences: Need brief. Since target includes references. Could include only source and maybe Pearl, Joyce, Nozick, Parfit? But avoid lengthy bibliography. Maybe references list 5-7 items. Need word count includes references. Need keep around 2400-3000. We need estimate. Could produce ~2700 words. Need be careful not too long. The final can be ~3000 max. We need count roughly. Let's aim 2600-2800 words. Since desired oververbosity 9, but user target 2400-3000. Need not exceed. Could be ~2800.\n\nNeed include equations nicely. No need LaTeX super complex but can use.\n\nNeed use A-FDT consistently. Be careful not call it FDT except maybe \"the source calls it functional decision theory\" once. But user says use A-FDT consistently. Maybe say \"The source paper calls the theory FDT; in this essay I refer to the action-level theory specified by Equation (4) as A-FDT.\" That uses FDT once but identifies source. Could be okay. But \"Use A-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name\". Maybe avoid calling it FDT except when quoting source? The source has \"functional decision theory (FDT)\". Need maybe say \"I will use A-FDT throughout; where the source says FDT, I am referring to the same formal rule under the assigned name.\" That is fine.\n\nNeed quotations faithful. If quote, keep exact. Could quote \"Which output of this decision procedure causes the best outcome?\" maybe. Need not many quotes. If quote \"Functional decision theory is to subjunctive dependence as causal decision theory is to causal dependence.\" But if we use A-FDT, quote source maybe \"Functional decision theory is to subjunctive dependence...\" but then maybe not. Need faithful. Could avoid quotes. But user says keep quotations faithful. Use few.\n\nNeed think about exact Equation (4) source: It writes \\FDT(P, G, \\obs) \\coloneqq \\argmax_{\\act \\in \\mathcal A} \\EE\\left(\\VV \\cond \\Do\\left(\\FDTvar(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)= \\act\\right)\\right). Need reproduce adapted:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{A\\text{-}FDT}(P,G,x) := \\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A} \\mathbb E\\bigl[V \\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a)\\bigr].\n\\]\nNeed note x maybe observation. Use do not Do maybe.\n\nNeed maybe include equation for EDT and CDT for comparison:\nEDT: argmax E[V | Obs=x, Act=a]\nCDT: argmax E[V | do(Act=a), Obs=x]\nA-FDT: as above. This helps. Need explain that all are expected utility maximization but differ in hypothetical connective.\n\nNeed discuss policies:\nSource footnote 7: preferred formalization agents iterate over policies rather than actions. But for assignment Eq (4) action. Need maybe say \"A-FDT as defined by Equation (4) evaluates single actions. When a problem involves several observation points or other agents who predict a whole strategy, the natural operational extension is to let the intervened variable be a policy.\" Could define:\nLet \\Pi be variable for A-FDT policy mapping observations to actions. Policy A-FDT:\n\\[\n\\Pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi E[V | do(\\Pi=\\pi)], \\quad \\text{then act } \\Pi^*(x).\n\\]\nThis is not explicitly Eq (4) but follows from its logic; source says preferred formulation uses policies. Need distinguish.\n\nPotential issue: \"action functional decision theory\" maybe could mean evaluating actions as functions? Maybe not. Need ensure not confuse. Use \"action functional\" as assigned.\n\nNeed think of \"how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals\" thoroughly. Need describe counterfactuals: A-FDT counterfactual \"if the output of this very decision function had been a, the world would be as G says given that logical fact.\" It is not \"if I physically did a while my decision function remained actual\" (CDT) nor \"if I learned I did a\" (EDT). In Newcomb, CDT counterfactual holds prediction fixed; A-FDT changes prediction. In lesion, A-FDT holds lesion fixed; EDT changes lesion evidence. In transparent, A-FDT may change observation.\n\nNeed maybe include \"type-token\" distinction: CDT intervenes on token action; A-FDT intervenes on type/decision function. Source mentions Peirce. Could include.\n\nNeed perhaps discuss self-reference and recursion theorem: P must contain variable for output of A-FDT run on P,G,x; Kleene recursion theorem/quine makes self-reference harmless. Need mention but not dwell.\n\nNeed maybe discuss bounded agents: Source says idealized models; real agents approximate. Could mention briefly.\n\nNeed ensure no unsupported claims: If say A-FDT always gets highest utility, qualify source claims in fair dilemmas and no proof. Need mention no general optimality proof.\n\nNeed final conclusion brief.\n\nLet's draft with word count in mind.\n\nPotential final essay:\n\nTitle\n\nParagraphs:\n1 intro: 200 words.\n2 EU/hypotheticals + equations: 300.\n3 A-FDT definition: 400.\n4 subjunctive graph: 350.\n5 examples Newcomb: 300.\n6 Smoking lesion: 250.\n7 Transparent/Parfit: 400.\n8 observation ambiguity/policies/open problems: 450.\n9 implications/conclusion: 200.\nReferences: 150. Total ~3000. Need maybe trim.\n\nLet's write carefully.\n\nNeed include references maybe:\n- Yudkowsky and Soares 2017? The source title Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality. arXiv:1710.05060v2.\n- Pearl 2000 Causality.\n- Joyce 1999 Foundations of Causal Decision Theory.\n- Nozick 1969 Newcomb.\n- Parfit 1986 Reasons and Persons.\nMaybe Skyrms 1980? But avoid lengthy. Maybe 5 refs.\n\nNeed maybe source has no authors in excerpt? It says Yudkowsky and Soares from preamble. Use.\n\nNeed think of exact word count. We can produce ~2700. Need maybe no appendices.\n\nLet's compose. Need use headings.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nIntro:\n\"A-FDT is a decision theory that treats the agent's choice as the output of a fixed mathematical function. The normative question is not 'Which physical act causes best outcome?' (CDT) nor 'Which act is best news?' (EDT), but 'Which output of this very decision function would yield the best outcome?'\" Good.\n\nFormal section:\nGeneric expected utility: EU(a)= sum P(a hookrightarrow o_j; x) U(o_j). The hook is decision hypothetical. EDT: P(o|x,a). CDT: P(o|do(Act=a),x). A-FDT: P(o|do(D_x=a)). Need maybe use notation.\n\nA-FDT definition:\nLet M=(P,G). P distribution over variables including Act, Obs, Outcome, V, and logical variables for computations. G dependency graph. x observation history. Let D_x = fdt(underline P, underline G, underline x) variable representing output of A-FDT on its actual inputs. Then equation. The do operation sets D_x=a and propagates through G. If G has structural equations, do replaces equation for D_x. Expected utility:\nEU_A(a;x)= E[V | do(D_x=a)]. Choose max. If ties any.\nObservation handling: Equation does not condition on Obs=x; x appears as input to D_x. This is crucial.\nSelf-reference: model includes variable naming itself; Kleene.\n\nSubjunctive dependence:\n\"When two systems compute same function or one reliably represents the other's computation, their outputs subjunctively depend on that function.\" Causal dependence special case; statistical correlation not. G must encode these. Operational rules. Need maybe write graph examples.\n\nExamples:\nNewcomb with numbers. Use D for A-FDT output. Equations:\nEU_A(one)=0.99*1,000,000+